128	<section id="landed" class="part">
129	<h2>What landed before</h2>
130	<p>Every item of the earlier page of skill drafts landed. The last two of its commits were <code>f898915bc</code>, which landed version 10 at 11:15 UTC on 5 November, and <code>8fa68948f</code>, which fixed its cold read's flags at 11:23. This page starts the history afresh, at your word.</p>
131	</section>
132	
133	<section id="s1" class="part">
134	<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
135	<p>Fourteen items, in two groups. Items 1 to 6 are draft 12. You approved it at 11:53 UTC on 5 November, and commit <code>23f18f024</code> landed it at 11:54. A cold read of it, read as the coordinating session, found 12 flags, and at 11:57 the skill writer's commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> fixed the text. These items show the text as that commit left it, against the words that you approved on version 11 of the earlier page. Items 7 to 14 are draft 13. You approved it at 12:19 with a caveat: the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes is only a surface for you to check the changes, it shows landed changes only, and nothing in that skill waits on you. Commit <code>e6bc2b83c</code> landed it at 12:21. A cold read found 13 flags, and at 12:27 the skill writer's commit <code>b9952aba9</code> fixed the text. These items show the text on <code>main</code> now, against the words that you approved on version 2 of this page. Two parts of draft 13 did not land, by your caveat: steps 4 and 6 of the method stay as they were.</p>
136	<p>In each item, the words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. Where the approved draft did not show the text, the item says so, and the diff is against the text before. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>
137	
138	<section id="l1" class="part item">
139	<h3><span class="num">Change 1 &middot; SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, the second bullet</span>The report of a landed change, and its reversal</h3>
140	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
141	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, line 125</p>
142	<ul><li>After a change lands, have a worker update the page of skill drafts, whose link the boot note holds (<code>references/skill-rewrite.md</code>, “The method”, step 7). <span class="cut">Then</span> <span class="add">When the worker is done,</span> tell the curator in one line, in your next message, with the <span class="cut">page's link.</span> <span class="add">commit and the page's link. Watch the page for comments sent to Claude.</span> If the curator asks to reverse a change, <span class="cut">by a comment on its item or in chat,</span> have the skill writer restore the old words <span class="cut">at once,</span> <span class="add">when the curator asks, by a comment sent to Claude on the item or in chat,</span> with no cold read. <span class="add">The reversal does not wait for the end of the curator's reading.</span> A change that nobody reads can weaken how you and the curator work together, and nobody notices.</li></ul>
143	</div></div>
144	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The bullet landed at your approval of 11:53, in commit <code>23f18f024</code>. A cold read then found three problems in it, and commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> fixed them. The one line in chat did not say what it names, or whether it waits for the worker; now it is sent when the worker is done, and names the commit and the page. Nothing made a reversal comment on the page reach the session; now the session watches the page for comments sent to Claude, a sentence that the skill writer added. A reversal comment could be held until your reading ended, since the session holds a page's comments while you read; now the reversal is made when you ask, and does not wait for the end of your reading.</p></div>
145	</section>
146	
147	<section id="l2" class="part item">
148	<h3><span class="num">Change 2 &middot; SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, the third bullet</span>A waiting change goes under “Proposed changes”</h3>
149	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
150	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, line 126</p>
151	<ul><li>A change that touches the curator's role and decisions (this section), the asks, the held list, the review list or the register in which you write to the curator (the bullets at the head of “Talking to the curator”) waits for the curator's yes to its exact words. So does every rewrite of <code>SKILL.md</code> or of a whole part, since it can touch any rule. Put it as an item under <span class="cut">the page's proposed changes.</span> <span class="add">“Proposed changes” on the page of skill drafts.</span> Such a change has no default: it stays out of the skill until the yes. If you are unsure whether a change touches these, it waits.</li></ul>
152	</div></div>
153	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The bullet landed at your approval of 11:53, in commit <code>23f18f024</code>. Its words “the page's” leaned on the bullet before it, so a reader could not be sure which page was meant. Commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> names the page of skill drafts and its section “Proposed changes”.</p></div>
154	</section>
155	
156	<section id="l3" class="part item">
157	<h3><span class="num">Change 3 &middot; SKILL.md, “Pages”, a new bullet</span>The form of a landed item</h3>
158	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
159	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, “Pages”, line 200, after the list of what an item holds; version 11 did not show it, and the text before had none</p>
160	<ul><li><span class="add">On the page of skill drafts, an item of a change that landed holds its new text whole, with the old words shown struck through and the new words marked, and its reason inside the item's Select range. It has no collapsed current text.</span></li></ul>
161	</div></div>
162	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> added it. Step 7 asked for the old words struck through, while “Pages” asked for the current text collapsed, which after a landing is the same as the new text. A worker briefed from both could not tell which rule wins. Now “Pages” says what a landed item holds. This changes the reading, at your approval of 11:53, that “Pages” stays as it is.</p></div>
163	</section>
164	
165	<section id="l4" class="part item">
166	<h3><span class="num">Change 4 &middot; skill-rewrite.md, “The method”, step 7</span>What the page of skill drafts holds</h3>
167	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
168	<p class="where-in">skill-rewrite.md, “The method”, lines 19 to 21</p>
169	<p>7. For the coordinator skill, after each <span class="cut">landing,</span> <span class="add">change lands,</span> have a worker update the page of skill drafts. <span class="add">Give it the page's URL from the boot note, and have it publish by that URL.</span> The page has two sections:</p>
170	<ul><li>Landed changes: one item for each change <span class="cut">of the last landing. The item gives the new words, with the old words struck through, against the text that the curator approved</span> <span class="add">that landed since the curator's last approval</span> on the page, or <span class="cut">else the text before the change.</span> <span class="add">since the curator last said that they had read it, so that no change lands unseen while the curator is away. The item takes the form of a landed item in <code>SKILL.md</code>, “Pages”. For a change that waited for the curator's yes, it shows the change against the words that the curator approved. For any other change, it shows the change against the skill's text before it.</span> It gives the change's reason, such as a cold read's flag or <span class="cut">the lint.</span> <span class="add">a hit of the skills' lint, <code>explorations/coordinator/tools/skills-lint.py</code>.</span></li><li>Proposed changes: the items that wait for the curator's yes. <span class="add">An approved item leaves this section when it lands, and appears under the landed changes.</span></li></ul>
171	</div></div>
172	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The step landed at your approval of 11:53, in commit <code>23f18f024</code>. A cold read then found six problems in it, and commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> fixed them. “After each landing” could mean a batch's landing, which is what the word means elsewhere in this skill; now the step says “after each change lands”. With only the last landing's changes on the page, a change could drop off the page while you were away; now the page keeps every change since your last approval or your last word that you read it. A worker given no URL makes a new page; now the step gives it the page's URL from the boot note. The text that a diff is set against read two ways; now a change that waited for your yes is shown against the words that you approved, and any other change against the text before it, in the form that “Pages” gives. An approved item could stay under “Proposed changes” after it landed; now it moves to the landed changes. “The lint” was named nowhere else in the skill; now it is named by its path.</p></div>
173	</section>
174	
175	<section id="l5" class="part item">
176	<h3><span class="num">Change 5 &middot; skill-rewrite.md, “The method”, the bullet after the steps</span>A worker keeps the page in step</h3>
177	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
178	<p class="where-in">skill-rewrite.md, “The method”, line 24</p>
179	<ul><li>The writer edits and commits the skill files. You push, and keep the page of the change in step: the part's page for the other skills, the page of skill drafts for the coordinator <span class="cut">skill</span> <span class="add">skill, by a worker</span> (step 7).</li></ul>
180	</div></div>
181	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The bullet landed at your approval of 11:53, in commit <code>23f18f024</code>. It said that you keep the page in step, while step 7 and <code>SKILL.md</code> have a worker update it. Commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> makes it agree: the page is kept in step by a worker.</p></div>
182	</section>
183	
184	<section id="l6" class="part item">
185	<h3><span class="num">Change 6 &middot; sources.md, “Changes landed on 2026-11-05”</span>The provenance of draft 12 and its fixes</h3>
186	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
187	<p class="where-in">sources.md, “Changes landed on 2026-11-05”, two new last paragraphs, all new: version 11 did not show them, and the text before had none</p>
188	<p><span class="add">The curator's approval in chat, 2026-11-05, 11:53 UTC (“proposal approved”), of the drafts page's version 11, draft 12, from his rule of 11:5x UTC that the report of landed skill changes goes on the page of skill drafts, not in chat:</span></p>
189	<ul><li><span class="add"><code>SKILL.md</code>, “The curator's role and decisions”, the report bullet: after a change lands, a worker updates the page of skill drafts, and the chat gives one line and the page's link; a reversal may come by a comment on the change's item. The waiting bullet: its item goes under the page's proposed changes.</span></li><li><span class="add"><code>skill-rewrite.md</code>, “The method”, step 7: the page's two sections, the landed changes of the last landing as diffs with their reasons, and the proposed changes; the bullet after the steps points to step 7. POSITIONS changes stay reported in chat (“Quiet times”), and “Pages” stays as it is, the struck-through words being its marking of a change.</span></li></ul>
190	<p><span class="add">The cold read of <code>23f18f024</code>, 2026-11-05, read as the coordinating session, changed the lines above; its flags settled by the coordinator:</span></p>
191	<ul><li><span class="add"><code>skill-rewrite.md</code>, “The method”, step 7: “after each change lands” in place of “after each landing”, since a landing is a batch's; the landed section keeps every change since the curator's last approval or last word that he has read the page, in place of the last landing's changes only; the worker publishes by the page's URL from the boot note; an approved item moves from “Proposed changes” to the landed changes when it lands; each change shown against the words he approved, or else the text before it; the skills' lint named by its path. The bullet after the steps: the page kept in step by a worker.</span></li><li><span class="add"><code>SKILL.md</code>, “The curator's role and decisions”: the one-line report names the commit and the page, and is sent when the worker is done; the page is watched for comments sent to Claude; a reversal is made when the curator asks, by such a comment or in chat, with no wait for the end of his reading; a waiting change goes under “Proposed changes” on the page of skill drafts.</span></li><li><span class="add"><code>SKILL.md</code>, “Pages”: one line for the item of a landed change, its new text whole with the old words struck through and the new words marked, its reason inside the Select range, and no collapsed current text. This changes the reading of 11:53 that “Pages” stays as it is.</span></li></ul>
192	
193	</div></div>
194	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Commit <code>23f18f024</code> added the first paragraph and its two lines, and <code>f1dff89b4</code> the second paragraph and its three lines. The file records where each change of the skill came from, for its maintenance. A line of it is not a rule, so it takes no cold read.</p></div>
195	</section>
196	
197	<section id="l7" class="part item">
198	<h3><span class="num">Change 7 &middot; SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, the second bullet</span>One bullet for the report and the reversal on both pages</h3>
